window.registerTopic('react-fund', {
  why: `For a React developer this is the main event. Interviewers start with **what React is and why** (declarative, component-based, Virtual DOM, one-way data flow), then test the **mental model**: elements vs components, props vs state, why state must not be mutated, how events and keys work, controlled vs uncontrolled inputs, and the component lifecycle. The follow-ups are about **correctness**: why index keys break lists, why \`setState\` takes a function, what \`super(props)\` is for, how reconciliation decides what to update.

They are listening for precise vocabulary used correctly, plus the reasons behind the rules.`,
  notes: [
    { h: 'What React is', md: `React is an open-source **front-end JavaScript library** (not a full framework) created by **Jordan Walke** at Facebook, first deployed on News Feed in 2011 and Instagram in 2012, with roots in the PHP extension XHP. It builds **composable user interfaces** declaratively. Major features: **JSX**, the **Virtual DOM**, **server-side rendering**, **unidirectional data flow**, reusable **components**.

Advantages: performance via the Virtual DOM, readable JSX, client and server rendering, easy integration with other frameworks, easy unit and integration testing with Jest. Limitations: it is only the view layer (you add routing, state management), a learning curve, JSX can feel odd, and the ecosystem moves fast. React Native brings the same model to mobile.` },
    { h: 'JSX, elements and components', md: `**JSX** (JavaScript XML) is syntactic sugar for \`React.createElement(type, props, ...children)\`; browsers cannot run it, so Babel (or the new JSX transform, which removes the need to import React) compiles it. It is stricter than HTML: \`className\` instead of \`class\` and \`htmlFor\` instead of \`for\` (both reserved words), camelCase attributes and event names, every tag closed, one root (or a Fragment). Expressions go in \`{}\`; attribute values cannot be interpolated inside quotes; loops use \`array.map\`, not \`for\`. JSX escapes embedded values, which prevents injection attacks; \`dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html }}\` is the deliberate escape hatch. Comments are \`{/* … */}\`.

An **element** is a plain, immutable object describing what to render (\`{ type: 'div', props: { id, children } }\`); a **component** is a function or class that takes props and returns elements. Component names must start with a **capital letter** so JSX treats them as components rather than HTML tags. You can use React without JSX by calling \`createElement\` directly, and without rendering HTML by returning \`null\`, strings, numbers, arrays or fragments.` },
    { h: 'Function vs class components', md: `A **function component** is a JavaScript function receiving \`props\` and returning JSX; since Hooks (React 16.8) it can hold state and effects and is the recommended form. **Class components** extend \`React.Component\` with a \`render()\` method (the only required method), hold state in \`this.state\`, bind handlers (constructor \`bind\`, class-field arrows, or inline arrows) and use lifecycle methods. Reasons to still write a class: error boundaries (\`componentDidCatch\`, \`getDerivedStateFromError\`) and legacy code. Differences: syntax, state (\`useState\` vs \`this.state\`), lifecycle (effects vs methods), props access (\`props\` vs \`this.props\`), and no \`this\` binding in functions.

**Stateless** components depend only on props; **stateful** components own state. **Pure components** render the same output for the same props and state: \`React.memo(Component)\` for functions (shallow props comparison), \`React.PureComponent\` for classes (shallow props and state in \`shouldComponentUpdate\`).

Constructor rules: call \`super(props)\` first so \`this.props\` is available inside the constructor; initialise state with \`this.state = {…}\` (never \`setState\` in the constructor); the constructor runs once per mounted instance because reconciliation reuses the instance when the same component appears in the same place. A constructor is optional when you neither initialise state nor bind methods; class fields let you skip it.` },
    { h: 'Props and state', md: `**Props** are read-only inputs passed from parent to child like HTML attributes (\`<Child name="John" />\` → \`props.name\`); a component must never modify its own props (React calls such components "pure" in that sense). \`children\` is the prop holding whatever is between the opening and closing tags. **Default props** fill in \`undefined\` props (not \`null\`); a boolean prop with no value defaults to \`true\`. Validate props in development with **PropTypes** (\`PropTypes.arrayOf(PropTypes.shape({...}))\`, union types with \`oneOfType\`), or statically with TypeScript or Flow.

**State** is private, owned data that changes over the component's lifetime; changing it re-renders the component and its children. Rules: never mutate it directly (\`this.state.x = 1\` does not re-render; use \`setState\` or the hook setter); \`setState\` is **asynchronous and batched**, so pass a function \`setState(prev => ({ count: prev.count + 1 }))\` when the new value depends on the old; class \`setState\` **merges** into the existing object (\`replaceState\` replaced it), hook setters **replace**; a callback as the second argument runs after the update. Update objects immutably with spread or \`Object.assign\`; remove array items with \`filter\`; use computed keys \`{ [name]: value }\` for dynamic fields. Copying props into initial state is an anti-pattern because later prop changes are ignored. **Lifting state up** moves shared state to the closest common ancestor; **prop drilling** is passing props through components that do not need them (fix with composition or context).` },
    { h: 'Events', md: `React wraps native events in **SyntheticEvent**, a cross-browser wrapper with the same API (\`preventDefault\`, \`stopPropagation\`, \`nativeEvent\`). Differences from HTML: camelCase names (\`onClick\`), you pass a **function** not a string, you must call \`preventDefault()\` explicitly (returning false does nothing), and you pass the handler without parentheses (\`onClick={handleClick}\`; \`onClick={handleClick()}\` calls it on every render). Pass arguments with an arrow (\`onClick={() => handle(id)}\`) or \`bind\`, remembering that inline arrows create a new function per render and can defeat memoisation. React supports **pointer events** (\`onPointerDown\` …) for mouse, touch and pen. Event handlers passed as props let children notify parents.` },
    { h: 'Lists, keys and conditional rendering', md: `Render lists with \`map\`; give each sibling a **stable, unique key** so React can match items across renders. Index keys break when the list is reordered, filtered or edited (state sticks to the wrong row); they are safe only for static lists without ids that never reorder. Keys need to be unique among siblings, not globally; **keyed fragments** use \`<React.Fragment key>\` when each item renders multiple nodes.

Conditional rendering: \`if\`/ternary outside JSX, \`&&\` short-circuiting inside (beware \`0 && …\` renders 0), or return \`null\` to render nothing. Falsy values such as \`false\`, \`null\`, \`undefined\` and \`true\` render nothing; convert to strings to show them. Conditional classes: build the string outside quotes or with template literals. A **switching component** maps a prop to one of several components with an object lookup.` },
    { h: 'Forms, refs and the DOM', md: `**Controlled components** keep the input value in React state and update it in \`onChange\`, so React is the single source of truth and you can validate or transform on every keystroke. **Uncontrolled components** let the DOM hold the value and read it with a **ref** when needed (\`defaultValue\` sets the initial value). **Refs** give direct access to a DOM node or class instance for focus, text selection, media playback, imperative animations and third-party libraries; create them with \`React.createRef()\` or the \`useRef\` hook, or a callback ref; string refs are legacy. **Forward refs** pass a ref through a component to a child (needed for HOCs and component libraries); \`findDOMNode\` is discouraged. Focus an input on mount with \`autoFocus\` or \`ref.current.focus()\` in \`componentDidMount\`/\`useEffect\`.

Styles: \`style\` takes an object with camelCased keys (\`{ backgroundColor: 'red' }\`), merged with spread; vendor prefixes are manual; CSS Modules, styled-components or plain CSS files are common. Custom DOM attributes are passed through since React 16; spreading unknown props onto DOM elements causes warnings, so destructure what you need.` },
    { h: 'Virtual DOM, reconciliation and Fiber', md: `The **Virtual DOM** is an in-memory tree of React elements. On every state change React re-renders the tree in memory, **diffs** it against the previous tree and applies only the minimal set of real DOM changes. **Reconciliation** is that process; the **diffing algorithm** works in O(n) using two rules: elements of different types rebuild the subtree, and keys identify which children moved. **Shadow DOM** is unrelated: a browser feature for scoping web-component styles. Real vs Virtual DOM: real updates are slow and expensive, virtual updates are cheap and batched.

**Fiber** is the reconciliation engine rewritten in React 16: it splits rendering into units of work that can be paused, prioritised, aborted and resumed, enabling incremental rendering, animation smoothness and **concurrent rendering**. The **data flow** is one-way: parent to child via props.` },
    { h: 'Lifecycle (class components)', md: `Three phases: **mounting**, **updating**, **unmounting** (plus error handling). Mount order: \`constructor\` → \`static getDerivedStateFromProps\` → \`render\` → \`componentDidMount\` (fetch data, subscribe, DOM measurement). Update order: \`getDerivedStateFromProps\` → \`shouldComponentUpdate\` → \`render\` → \`getSnapshotBeforeUpdate\` (capture scroll position; its return value is the third argument to) → \`componentDidUpdate\` (compare prev props/state, listen to state changes). Unmount: \`componentWillUnmount\` (clear timers, unsubscribe; never \`setState\` here). Error methods: \`static getDerivedStateFromError\` and \`componentDidCatch\`.

Deprecated since 16.3 and prefixed \`UNSAFE_\`: \`componentWillMount\`, \`componentWillReceiveProps\`, \`componentWillUpdate\`. Make AJAX calls in \`componentDidMount\` (or \`useEffect\`), not the constructor or render. Force a re-render without setState with \`forceUpdate()\` (rarely appropriate). Recommended method ordering: statics, constructor, lifecycle methods, event handlers, render helpers, \`render\`. \`displayName\` names a component for DevTools; \`isMounted\` and mixins are anti-patterns.` },
    { h: 'react-dom, SSR and project setup', md: `\`react\` holds the core (createElement, Component, hooks, Children); \`react-dom\` holds DOM-specific APIs (\`render\`/\`createRoot\`, \`hydrate\`, \`createPortal\`, \`unmountComponentAtNode\`, \`findDOMNode\`); the split (since v0.14) lets React target native, art and other renderers. \`ReactDOMServer\` provides \`renderToString\`, \`renderToStaticMarkup\` and \`renderToNodeStream\` for **server-side rendering**, which speeds first paint and helps SEO; the client then hydrates. **Create React App** (react-scripts) gives a zero-config setup with Babel, Webpack, ESLint, Jest, hot reload, TypeScript support and polyfill options (core-js or react-app-polyfill); set \`HTTPS=true\` for https and \`NODE_PATH\`/\`jsconfig\` baseUrl to avoid relative imports. Enable **production mode** with \`NODE_ENV=production\` via Webpack's DefinePlugin to strip PropTypes checks and warnings. Folder structures: by feature/route or by file type. Check the running version with \`React.version\`. ES6 is not strictly required to learn React, but destructuring, spread, classes, arrow functions and template literals appear everywhere.` },
  ],
  pitfalls: [
    'Mutating state (`state.items.push(x)`) and wondering why nothing re-renders. Create a new array or object.',
    'Using array index as key for lists that can reorder or delete. Component state and inputs stick to the wrong rows.',
    'Calling the handler in JSX: `onClick={save()}` runs on render. Pass the function: `onClick={save}` or `onClick={() => save(id)}`.',
    'Describing the Virtual DOM as "faster than the DOM" without the real point: it lets React compute minimal batched updates.',
    'Forgetting `super(props)`; `this.props` is undefined inside the constructor without it.',
    'Confusing controlled and uncontrolled inputs, or mixing `value` with `defaultValue`.',
  ],
  model: [
    { q: 'What is the difference between state and props?', a: 'Props are inputs passed from a parent to a child; they are read-only inside the child and change only when the parent re-renders with new values. State is data a component owns and manages itself with setState or a hook setter; changing it re-renders the component and its subtree. Props flow down to configure components; state holds what changes over time. Lift state up when two siblings need the same data.', ref: 'react-10_2' },
    { q: 'What is the Virtual DOM and how does reconciliation work?', a: 'The Virtual DOM is an in-memory representation of the UI as a tree of React elements. When state or props change, React renders a new tree, diffs it against the previous one and computes the smallest set of real DOM operations, then applies them in a batch. The diff is linear-time thanks to two heuristics: elements of different types replace their whole subtree, and keys tell React which list items are the same across renders. Fiber is the implementation that lets this work be split and prioritised.', ref: 'react-41' },
    { q: 'Why should keys not be array indexes?', a: 'React uses keys to decide which list items persisted between renders. With index keys, inserting, removing or reordering shifts every index, so React thinks items changed when they moved and re-uses the wrong component instances; state such as input text stays attached to the wrong row and performance drops. Use a stable id from the data; indexes are acceptable only for static lists that never reorder.', ref: 'react-64' },
    { q: 'Why pass a function to setState?', a: 'setState is asynchronous and batched, so several calls in one event may be merged and `this.state` may be stale when you read it. The functional form `setState(prev => ({ count: prev.count + 1 }))` receives the latest pending state and guarantees sequential updates compose correctly. The same applies to hook setters.', ref: 'react-82' },
  ],
  quiz: [
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'What does JSX compile to?', options: ['HTML strings', 'React.createElement calls', 'Template literals', 'Web Components'], answer: 1, explain: 'JSX is sugar for `React.createElement(type, props, ...children)`; Babel performs the transform.', ref: 'react-4' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'Why does React use `className` instead of `class`?', options: ['For performance', '`class` is a reserved word in JavaScript', 'Historical accident', 'To support CSS Modules'], answer: 1, explain: 'JSX is JavaScript, so reserved words cannot be attribute names; same for `htmlFor`.', ref: 'react-45' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'What happens if you assign `this.state.message = "x"` directly?', options: ['The component re-renders', 'A runtime error is thrown', 'State changes but no re-render is scheduled', 'The state is frozen'], answer: 2, explain: 'Only setState schedules an update; direct mutation is invisible to React.', ref: 'react-11' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'A component whose input value lives in React state and updates on every change is…', options: ['Uncontrolled', 'Controlled', 'Pure', 'Stateless'], answer: 1, explain: 'Controlled components make React the single source of truth; uncontrolled ones read the DOM via refs.', ref: 'react-29' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'Which lifecycle method is the right place for an AJAX call in a class?', options: ['constructor', 'render', 'componentDidMount', 'componentWillUnmount'], answer: 2, explain: 'The component is mounted and can safely set state; in functions use useEffect.', ref: 'react-127' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'What is a SyntheticEvent?', options: ['A fake event used only in tests', 'A cross-browser wrapper around the native event with the same interface', 'An event emitted by Redux', 'A custom DOM event'], answer: 1, explain: 'Access the raw event via `nativeEvent`.', ref: 'react-16' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'When is it safe to use the index as a key?', options: ['Always', 'Never', 'When the list is static, items have no ids, and it is never reordered or filtered', 'Only for numbers'], answer: 2, explain: 'All three conditions must hold.', ref: 'react-267' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'What does `super(props)` do in a class constructor?', options: ['Creates state', 'Makes `this.props` available inside the constructor', 'Binds all methods', 'Registers the component with React'], answer: 1, explain: 'Without passing props, `this.props` is undefined only inside the constructor.', ref: 'react-90' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'Which is a true statement about React elements?', options: ['They are mutable DOM nodes', 'They are plain objects describing what to render and are immutable once created', 'They must be classes', 'They hold state'], answer: 1, explain: 'Elements are cheap descriptions; components produce them.', ref: 'react-5' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'How does `React.memo` decide whether to re-render?', options: ['Deep comparison of props', 'Shallow comparison of previous and next props', 'It always re-renders', 'It compares state only'], answer: 1, explain: 'Pass a custom comparison function as the second argument when needed.', ref: 'react-8' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'Which React 16 change affects uncaught render errors?', options: ['They are logged and ignored', 'They unmount the whole component tree unless an error boundary catches them', 'They reload the page', 'They are converted to warnings'], answer: 1, explain: 'React prefers removing a corrupted UI to leaving it in place.', ref: 'react-235' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'What does `getSnapshotBeforeUpdate` return value become?', options: ['The new state', 'The third argument of componentDidUpdate', 'A prop', 'Nothing; it is ignored'], answer: 1, explain: 'Typical use: capture scroll position before the DOM changes.', ref: 'react-77' },
    { type: 'short', q: 'Explain the difference between an element and a component.', a: 'An element is an immutable plain object (`{ type, props }`) that describes a DOM node or component instance to render; creating one is cheap. A component is a function or class that accepts props and returns elements (a tree). Components produce elements; React turns elements into DOM.', ref: 'react-5' },
    { type: 'short', q: 'List the mounting lifecycle order for a class component.', a: 'constructor → static getDerivedStateFromProps → render → componentDidMount. Side effects such as data fetching and subscriptions go in componentDidMount.', ref: 'react-74' },
    { type: 'short', q: 'How do events in React differ from HTML events?', a: 'camelCase names (onClick), a function is passed instead of a string, preventDefault must be called explicitly (returning false does nothing), the handler is referenced without parentheses, and the event object is a SyntheticEvent wrapper that normalises browsers.', ref: 'react-13' },
    { type: 'short', q: 'What are controlled and uncontrolled components, and when would you pick each?', a: 'Controlled: value in React state, updated via onChange; use for validation, formatting, or when other UI depends on the value. Uncontrolled: the DOM owns the value, read with a ref on submit; use for simple forms, file inputs, or integrating non-React code. defaultValue sets the initial uncontrolled value.', ref: 'react-30' },
  ],
  cards: [
    { f: 'React in one sentence', b: 'An open-source JavaScript library for building composable, declarative UIs, created by Jordan Walke at Facebook (2011 News Feed, 2012 Instagram).', ref: 'react-1' },
    { f: 'Major features (5)', b: 'JSX, Virtual DOM, server-side rendering, one-way data flow, reusable components.', ref: 'react-3' },
    { f: 'JSX is…', b: 'Sugar for React.createElement(type, props, ...children); compiled by Babel; stricter than HTML.', ref: 'react-4' },
    { f: 'Element vs component', b: 'Element: immutable object describing UI. Component: function/class returning elements.', ref: 'react-5' },
    { f: 'Props vs state', b: 'Props: read-only inputs from parent. State: owned, mutable via setState; triggers re-render.', ref: 'react-10_2' },
    { f: 'Why setState(fn)?', b: 'setState is async and batched; the functional form gets the latest pending state.', ref: 'react-82' },
    { f: 'Pure component', b: 'Same output for same props/state. React.memo (functions, shallow props) / PureComponent (classes, shallow props+state).', ref: 'react-8' },
    { f: 'Controlled vs uncontrolled', b: 'Controlled: state + onChange. Uncontrolled: DOM value read via ref; defaultValue for initial.', ref: 'react-29' },
    { f: 'Key prop', b: 'Stable unique id among siblings so React can match list items; avoid indexes for dynamic lists.', ref: 'react-18' },
    { f: 'SyntheticEvent', b: 'Cross-browser wrapper around native events; same API; nativeEvent for the raw one.', ref: 'react-16' },
    { f: 'Virtual DOM steps', b: 'Re-render tree in memory → diff with previous → apply minimal real DOM updates.', ref: 'react-25' },
    { f: 'Diffing rules', b: 'Different element types rebuild the subtree; keys identify moved children. O(n).', ref: 'react-288' },
    { f: 'React Fiber', b: 'React 16 reconciler: incremental rendering, work can be paused, prioritised, aborted (concurrent rendering).', ref: 'react-27' },
    { f: 'Mount lifecycle order', b: 'constructor → getDerivedStateFromProps → render → componentDidMount.', ref: 'react-74' },
    { f: 'Update lifecycle order', b: 'getDerivedStateFromProps → shouldComponentUpdate → render → getSnapshotBeforeUpdate → componentDidUpdate.', ref: 'react-245' },
    { f: 'UNSAFE_ lifecycles', b: 'componentWillMount, componentWillReceiveProps, componentWillUpdate (deprecated since 16.3).', ref: 'react-75' },
    { f: 'Refs: when?', b: 'Focus/selection/media, imperative animations, third-party DOM libraries. createRef/useRef/callback refs; string refs legacy.', ref: 'react-289' },
    { f: 'react vs react-dom', b: 'react: core + hooks. react-dom: render/hydrate/createPortal/findDOMNode; split so React can target native.', ref: 'react-95' },
    { f: 'Lifting state up', b: 'Move shared state to the closest common ancestor and pass it down as props.', ref: 'react-32' },
    { f: 'Prop drilling', b: 'Passing props through intermediate components that do not need them; fix with composition or context.', ref: 'react-330' },
  ],
  coding: [
    { id: 'counter', title: 'Convert a class counter to a function component', prompt: 'Rewrite the class `Counter` (state `{ value: 0 }`, increment/decrement handlers) as a function component with `useState`, using the functional updater. Write JSX in the box and compare with the solution.', starter: '// class Counter extends React.Component {\n//   state = { value: 0 };\n//   inc = () => this.setState(p => ({ value: p.value + 1 }));\n//   render() { return <button onClick={this.inc}>{this.state.value}</button>; }\n// }\n\n// function Counter() { ... }\nconsole.log("write it, then reveal");', solution: 'import { useState } from "react";\n\nfunction Counter() {\n  const [value, setValue] = useState(0);\n  return (\n    <>\n      <button onClick={() => setValue(v => v - 1)}>-</button>\n      <span>{value}</span>\n      <button onClick={() => setValue(v => v + 1)}>+</button>\n    </>\n  );\n}', ref: 'react-226' },
    { id: 'controlled', title: 'Controlled input with uppercase transform', prompt: 'Write a function component with a controlled `<input>` whose value is always upper-cased, plus a submit handler that prevents the default and logs the value.', starter: '// function NameForm() {\n//   const [name, setName] = useState("");\n//   ...\n// }\nconsole.log("write it, then reveal");', solution: 'function NameForm() {\n  const [name, setName] = useState("");\n  const onSubmit = e => { e.preventDefault(); console.log(name); };\n  return (\n    <form onSubmit={onSubmit}>\n      <input value={name} onChange={e => setName(e.target.value.toUpperCase())} />\n      <button type="submit">Save</button>\n    </form>\n  );\n}', ref: 'react-29' },
    { id: 'immutable-update', title: 'Immutable state updates (plain JS, runs here)', prompt: 'Given `state = { user: { name: "A", tags: ["x"] }, count: 1 }`, produce a new state with name "B", tag "y" appended and count incremented, without mutating the original. Then prove the original is unchanged.', starter: 'const state = { user: { name: "A", tags: ["x"] }, count: 1 };\nconst next = /* your code */ state;\nconsole.log(JSON.stringify(state), JSON.stringify(next));', solution: 'const next = {\n  ...state,\n  count: state.count + 1,\n  user: { ...state.user, name: "B", tags: [...state.user.tags, "y"] },\n};', tests: [{ expr: 'next.user.tags.length === 2 && state.user.tags.length === 1 && next.count === 2 && next.user.name === "B"', expect: true }], ref: 'react-108' },
    { id: 'keys', title: 'Render a list with proper keys and a delete button', prompt: 'Write `TodoList({ todos, onDelete })` rendering `<li>` per todo with a stable key and a delete button that calls `onDelete(id)` without invoking it on render.', starter: '// function TodoList({ todos, onDelete }) {\n//   return <ul>{ ... }</ul>;\n// }\nconsole.log("write it, then reveal");', solution: 'function TodoList({ todos, onDelete }) {\n  return (\n    <ul>\n      {todos.map(t => (\n        <li key={t.id}>\n          {t.text}\n          <button onClick={() => onDelete(t.id)}>Delete</button>\n        </li>\n      ))}\n    </ul>\n  );\n}', notes: 'Key is the id, not the index. The arrow defers the call; `onClick={onDelete(t.id)}` would run during render.', ref: 'react-64' },
  ],
});
